  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 117 PM CDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nCool, dry high pressure settles southward tonight leading to lighter \nwinds and clearing skies. May be some mid/high level clouds so that \ncould offset some of the potential radiational cooling. The NBM 10th \npercentile does sneak down to about 36 in parts of southwest MN and \nnearby locations, but for now expect low temperatures around 38 or \nhigher with lower to mid 40s near and west of I-29. So, not a zero \npercent chance for patchy frost, but too low to issue any headlines.\n\nHigh pressure slowly moves east on Friday, so expecting some cool \nhigh temperatures. Highs generally in the low to mid 60s. Southerly \nflow increasing in the afternoon west of I-29, then across the \nentire area Friday night. Lows may need to be increased a bit into \nthe lower to mid 50s given the expected 925 mb temperatures.\n\nSaturday will be warmer with southerly flow turning southwest during \nthe afternoon. Highs more consistently in the upper 60s to lower 70s.\n\nA cool front moves through Saturday night into Sunday bringing cool \nand dry conditions.\n\nMonday will be dry and cool with highs in the 60s while southerly \nflow increases Tuesday into Wednesday ahead of the next wave, \nbringing a better potential for highs in the 70s.\n\nA series of weak, moisture starved systems will move through the \nNorthern Plains over the next 7 days, resulting in little to no \nimpacts. The better chances for sprinkles or very light showers will \nbe Friday and Saturday then again Monday into Tuesday. Both show \nwaves moving by to the north with the tail end of forcing scraping \nby the area. Little to no instability with each wave coupled with \nabundant dry air leaves little in the way of precipitation \nproduction potential. Friday and Saturday looks like the better \nchance for a spotty shower will be north of I-90 where better mid \nlevel saturation is expected.",
